/* A Knight of the Seven Kingdoms wiki — places archive. Original prose. */
Object.assign(WIKI_EXTRA, {

  // ================= The Reach: the heart of the tale =================

  "loc:ashford": {
    paras: [
      "Ashford is a modest river seat on the Cockleswhent, and its lord — a man history otherwise has no cause to remember — did the one thing that guaranteed his name would outlast his castle: he threw a tourney for his daughter and let the whole of the Seven Kingdoms show up. For a fortnight in 209 AC the little town below Ashford’s walls swelled past bursting, its inns doubled and trebled their custom, and its lord watched from the best seats in the realm as three of the blood royal, a future Hand of the King, and a hedge knight nobody had heard of before that week rearranged the succession in his honor.",
      "The castle itself stayed mostly a backdrop — the tourney’s business was done across the river, on the meadow that took the name and the legend — but Ashford’s hospitality is the thread that holds the story together administratively, if not emotionally. It was Lord Ashford’s law that ran during the fortnight, Lord Ashford’s master-of-games who set the lists, and Lord Ashford’s hall, presumably, where the ugly business of a prince’s broken fingers and a hedge knight’s blow was first argued over before anyone thought to call for seven champions a side. Afterward the castle went back to being what it had always been: a comfortable river seat that had, once, hosted the end of an age.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:ashford-meadow": {
    paras: [
      "The meadow lies across the Cockleswhent from the castle proper, a broad stretch of river-bottom grass that took two hundred pavilions and half the Reach’s chivalry without complaint in the spring of 209 AC. Lord Ashford meant the tourney purely as a showcase for his daughter’s hand — a fair maid, a field of champions, the usual arithmetic of a marriage market dressed up in silk and heraldry. What he got instead was the hinge on which the whole Targaryen century turns, delivered by a wandering puppeteer’s broken fingers and a hedge knight too tall and too plain-spoken to walk past them.",
      "The blow that Ser Duncan the Tall struck against Prince Aerion Targaryen for the puppeteer’s sake should, by every ordinary reckoning, have ended with Dunk’s head on a spike — striking a prince of the blood is treason under any name. What saved him was a law so old most of the lords on the meadow had to be reminded it existed: trial by seven champions a side, an Andal rite that calls the Warrior himself to judge, invoked so rarely that finding seven men willing to die for a stranger’s honor was nearly as hard as finding seven willing to kill him for insulting a prince. That the realm’s best knight volunteered for the losing side, on principle, is the detail the singers never let go of.",
      "The trial itself is a battle in miniature, fought in front of the whole assembled chivalry of the Reach and the stormlands, and it ends the only way such things end — with more paid than anyone meant to spend. Baelor Breakspear takes a mace blow struck for someone else and dies of it within days; the hedge knight keeps the hand and the foot the law would have taken from him for false witness or worse; and the tourney itself is never finished, its melee abandoned, its champions scattered to more urgent business than a lord’s daughter’s wedding. Ashford Meadow’s odd distinction is that nobody remembers who won it — only what it cost.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:standfast": {
    paras: [
      "Standfast is what remains when a great house loses a war and a crown’s favor in the same afternoon. The chequy lions of House Osgrey once styled themselves Marshals of the Northmarch and held every acre between Highgarden and the sea worth holding; two generations of attainder and bad luck later, Ser Eustace Osgrey — the last of his line with land enough to matter — commands a single crumbling towerhouse, a handful of smallfolk, and the memory of what his family used to be. His sons died at Redgrass Field fighting for the black dragon, a debt the crown never forgave and never quite forgot either.",
      "It is to Standfast that Ser Duncan the Tall comes hunting honest work in the drought year of 211 AC, and finds instead an old man’s dwindled pride, a scorched wood, and a war over water that could ruin what little the Osgreys have left. Dunk’s service here — his first true post as a sworn sword rather than a wandering hopeful — teaches him the ugliest lesson of knighthood: that the lord you serve may be righteous, or a fool, or both, and your oath does not come with a clause excusing the difference. The dispute with neighboring Coldmoat nearly burns Standfast’s last wood and its last credibility to the ground.",
      "It ends, as these things sometimes do in the marches, not with a siege but a wedding — Ser Eustace and the Red Widow of Coldmoat, ancient rivals, closing the account the old-fashioned way. Standfast’s towerhouse outlives its own crisis as a lesser seat gathered quietly into a greater one, the chequy lions preserved by exactly the alliance a prouder age of Osgreys would have refused to consider.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:coldmoat": {
    paras: [
      "Coldmoat is everything Standfast is not — a well-run, well-moneyed castle behind a proper moat, held by a woman the smallfolk of two baronies call the Red Widow with equal parts fear and respect. Lady Rohanne Webber has buried four husbands by the time the tales find her, and the countryside’s favorite explanation for her survival — poison, mostly whispered, never proven — says more about what an age like this expects of a woman who keeps her own accounts and wins her own disputes than it does about Rohanne herself. Her father’s will is the true trap: Coldmoat passes out of her hands unless she remarries within the year, whatever she might prefer.",
      "The drought dispute with Standfast is Coldmoat’s business at its least flattering — a dammed stream, a burned wood, accusations traded across the border by men who ought to know better — and it is managed, in the end, by Lady Rohanne herself rather than by the castellan she is unwise enough to trust with too much rope. Her resolution, marrying the old enemy at her own gate rather than a stranger chosen for her, is the single cleverest stroke in either tale: it keeps Coldmoat in her hands, ends the water war for good, and turns a feud into an alliance without a drop more blood spent than had already soaked into Wat’s Wood.",
      "Coldmoat’s longer shadow falls well past the drought year. The line Lady Rohanne founds afterward runs, by the twisting logic of Westerosi genealogy, straight into the great houses of the age readers actually know — proof, if the castle needed one, that a well-managed holding outlasts every lord who ever thought he could take it from a widow by force of reputation alone.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:wats-wood": {
    paras: [
      "Wat’s Wood is a strip of old timber on the disputed border between Standfast and Coldmoat, named for some long-dead woodward nobody remembers past the name, and it is the first casualty of the great drought that opens the Sworn Sword. Ser Eustace Osgrey loves the wood the particular way ruined men love the last thing they still own outright — proprietarily, past reason — and when the stream that keeps it green runs low under Coldmoat’s dam, the wood dries to tinder within a season, ready to burn at the first spark of the wider quarrel. Burn it eventually does, and the fire does more to force a reckoning between the two castles than either lord’s pride ever managed on its own. A parched wood between two proud houses turns out to be exactly the kind of small, flammable thing that starts a war — or, in this case, forces an end to one.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:cider-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Cider Hall sits where the Mander meets the Cockleswhent, seat of a Fossoway branch prosperous enough to name a castle for its orchards, and it enters the tale as the origin point of a joke the wider saga never lets die. At Ashford Meadow, Ser Steffon Fossoway — of the family’s other branch — argues loudly that an unproven hedge knight has no business in a tourney meant for men of quality; a Fossoway of Cider Hall takes the opposite view, and vouches for Dunk when it costs something to do so. The quarrel outlives the tourney and splits the house cleanly down the middle, red apple against green, a division the family never quite mends and the rest of Westeros never quite lets them forget.",
      "Cider Hall itself is unremarkable as castles go — a comfortable holding among the orchards of the upper Mander, more concerned with cider presses than politics — which is precisely what makes its accidental role in the Fossoway split so telling. It took one hedge knight, one insult, and one moment of decency at a tourney gate to hand a minor Reach house a permanent identity crisis; the red apple and the green apple both still fly, generations on, arguing without ever quite remembering that the argument began here.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:starpike": {
    paras: [
      "Starpike is the last of what was once three castles held by House Peake, and Lord Gormon Peake nurses the loss of the other two the way some men nurse an old wound that never sets right — with equal parts grief and appetite for restitution. His family bet on the black dragon in the First Blackfyre Rebellion and lost badly at Redgrass Field, and it is there, on that field forty years gone, that Lord Gormon himself struck down Roger, the squire of an unlanded hedge knight named Ser Arlan of Pennytree — a small cruelty that means nothing to Peake and everything, decades later, to the man who becomes Ser Arlan’s last squire’s master in turn.",
      "By the time of the Mystery Knight, Lord Gormon has traded open rebellion for something quieter and more patient: he is one of the architects gathered at Whitewalls under Lord Butterwell’s roof, hoping a second throw of the Blackfyre dice might win back what Redgrass Field cost his house. Starpike’s three lost castles are the ledger entry that explains everything about the man — a lord for whom the crown’s peace is not stability but an unpaid debt, and who is willing to gamble a kingdom’s peace to collect it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:highgarden": {
    paras: [
      "Highgarden is the Reach entire, distilled into one castle of white towers wound about with climbing roses — the seat Aegon the Conqueror granted to the Tyrells, stewards rather than blood of the fallen Gardener kings, in the settlement that still rankles houses with a better claim by birth and a worse one by loyalty. In Dunk and Egg’s age it belongs to Lord Leo Tyrell, called Longthorn, Warden of the South, and liege lord in one form or another to nearly every Reach house the tales touch — Ashford, Standfast, Coldmoat, Cider Hall, Starpike, Goldengrove, all of them ultimately answerable, however distantly, to Highgarden’s word.",
      "That authority mostly stays offstage in the novellas, which is itself the point: a well-run overlordship is one that never has to be invoked, and the water war between Standfast and Coldmoat resolves itself in the marches without ever troubling Highgarden’s court. The Reach’s great and small houses squabble, marry, and occasionally bleed within a peace that Highgarden guarantees simply by existing at the top of it — the quiet, unglamorous kind of power that outlasts every tourney champion the era produces.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:oldtown": {
    paras: [
      "Oldtown is the oldest city in Westeros and, in an age still counting the cost of a broken succession, its steadiest one — a port of septs, scholars, and the Hightower’s beacon fire, ruled by a family that has learned over centuries never to bet everything on a single throne. Its true business is the Citadel, the grey fortress-college where the maesters of the realm forge their chains link by link, and in this era that business is personal to the tale: Aemon Targaryen, elder brother to the boy squiring for a hedge knight on the roads to the north, studies there for a chain of his own, choosing a maester’s service over a throne he stands closer to than almost anyone alive.",
      "That choice — a Targaryen prince declining the crown in favor of grey wool and a raven — says as much about the era’s exhaustion with its own royal house as anything that happens at Ashford. Oldtown does not produce kings; it produces the men who advise them, heal them, and eventually record what they did, and Aemon’s decision to disappear into that quieter tradition is the sort of small, sane rebellion Oldtown has always made room for.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:storms-end": {
    paras: [
      "Storm’s End has never fallen to siege or storm in all the centuries since the Storm Kings raised it, and the Baratheons who hold it now — cousins of the dragon by Orys Baratheon’s marriage into the conquered Durrandon line — wear that unbroken record as proudly as any words. In Dunk and Egg’s age the castle’s honor rides at Ashford in the person of Ser Lyonel Baratheon, called the Laughing Storm, a lord who fights tourneys with a booming good humor that unnerves opponents more reliably than a scowl ever could.",
      "The castle’s drum-towered walls, curved to break the sea gales that would flatten a straighter fortress, are as much a statement of character as of engineering — the Baratheons of this era favor strength worn lightly, the same trait that makes the Laughing Storm memorable on a tourney field crowded with grimmer men. It is a house, and a castle, entirely comfortable with its own legend, which is not nothing in an age when so many older houses are still trying to live down theirs.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:summerhall": {
    paras: [
      "Summerhall is the dragons’ holiday house — a lightly walled palace in the Dornish Marches built for music and warm evenings rather than defense, the sort of seat a family builds only once it has stopped expecting to need walls at all. In this era it belongs to Prince Maekar Targaryen, third son of Daeron the Good, a hard and joyless man who nonetheless keeps his household in a palace built for gaiety — and it is from Summerhall’s stables and side doors that his fourth son, a bald boy with no patience for princely tedium, slips away disguised as nobody in particular to squire for a hedge knight he has only just met.",
      "That runaway prince is, of course, Egg, and Summerhall’s significance to the tale is entirely retrospective: a comfortable palace that a future king once found so stifling he ran from it in a servant’s castoffs. The castle’s own famous catastrophe — the fire that will one day gut it on the night a grandson is born beneath a bad omen — is still decades off in this telling, a shadow the story is careful not to let readers see falling yet, though it is already there to be noticed if one knows to look.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Stormlands =================

  "loc:blackhaven": {
    paras: [
      "Blackhaven is a marcher seat of black basalt on the Dornish border, home to House Dondarrion and their purple lightning bolt, a house whose whole character is shaped by centuries spent as the first line against Dornish raids before Daeron II’s marriage finally bought a real peace. Ser Manfred Dondarrion is among the lords who take Ser Duncan the Tall into service at Ashford Meadow — a practical gesture of goodwill that stops well short of vouching for him when Dunk’s honor is later put to the test, which tells its own quiet story about how far marcher caution extends even toward a knight a lord has just hired.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:nightsong": {
    paras: [
      "Nightsong belongs to House Caron, self-styled lords of the nightingales, whose seat sits where the Boneway begins its climb into the mountains toward Dorne — one more marcher castle built by generations who took the old border war with the Dornish seriously long before any king in King’s Landing did. The Carons’ fondness for their nightingale conceit sits oddly against the grim practical work their ancestors actually did holding this stretch of frontier, though by Dunk and Egg’s era, with Daeron II’s Dornish peace some decades settled, the name has had time to soften from battle-boast into simple heraldry.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:evenfall-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Evenfall Hall crowns the Sapphire Isle of Tarth, reputed by sailors and songs alike to be the loveliest island anywhere in the narrow sea — blue waters, green hills, and a castle that has the luxury, unlike most stormlands seats, of looking out on scenery rather than a border. House Tarth holds it quietly in this era, a minor lordship whose chief business with the wider realm is supplying the narrow sea’s shipping lanes with safe anchorage rather than supplying the tourney field with champions; Evenfall Hall’s claim to fame in Dunk and Egg’s age is simply that it is, by universal agreement, a very pleasant place to be forgotten by history for a while.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Crownlands =================

  "loc:kings-landing": {
    paras: [
      "King’s Landing in the reign of Daeron II the Good is a capital finally permitted to be dull, in the specific and valuable sense that a kingdom recovering from civil war finds dullness a luxury. Daeron ended the First Blackfyre Rebellion and then spent his remaining years doing the unglamorous work of keeping it ended — patient government, a Dornish peace bought with two royal marriages instead of another war, and a court that runs on maesters and septons rather than swords, at least on the surface. The Red Keep under his rule is a working palace rather than a battleground, which is precisely the condition that lets the realm’s deeper fractures fester quietly instead of erupting.",
      "By the time of Whitewalls those fractures belong to a different reign: Daeron is gone, his heir Baelor Breakspear died on Ashford Meadow, and the throne has passed first to the frail, bookish Aerys I and, behind him, to the small council’s most formidable member — Brynden Rivers, called Bloodraven, Aerys’s Hand and master of whisperers, who runs the intelligence of an entire kingdom out of the Red Keep’s chambers with a thoroughness that makes the Second Blackfyre Rebellion’s failure look, in hindsight, almost inevitable. The city itself barely appears on the page in these tales, and that absence is its own kind of presence — every road Dunk and Egg travel, every plot they stumble into, ultimately answers to decisions made within these walls, whether the boy squire realizes yet how close he one day sits to making them himself.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:dragonstone": {
    paras: [
      "Dragonstone is older than the Seven Kingdoms it eventually conquered — raised by dragonlord art on a smoking island a century before Aegon ever looked west, its towers carved to writhe and scream in shapes no mason since has managed to explain. In Daeron II’s reign the island castle is the customary seat of the Targaryen heir, and in these tales it passes hand to hand with the succession’s bad luck: Baelor Breakspear held it as Prince of Dragonstone until Ashford Meadow cost him his life, and afterward it descends to his son Valarr, a boy inheriting his father’s title years before he was meant to inherit anything at all.",
      "That inheritance turns out to be nearly as unlucky as the last — the Great Spring Sickness that follows hard on Ashford’s heels claims Valarr along with much of his generation, one more turn in a succession crisis that keeps handing Dragonstone to men who never get to grow old in it. The island itself remains what it always was through all this: an ancient, faintly alien fortress that outlasts every prince assigned to hold it, indifferent to a mainland dynasty’s bad decade.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:duskendale": {
    paras: [
      "Duskendale was a kingdom before it was a town — seat of its own line of petty kings in the age before Aegon’s dragons, and House Darklyn has never entirely let the memory go even after centuries of being merely one port among several on the crownlands coast. The town does steady trade as a stop between King’s Landing and the north road, respectable rather than remarkable, and its knightly ambitions in Dunk and Egg’s day run through Ser Donnel of Duskendale, who wears the white cloak of the Kingsguard at Ashford Meadow — proof that whatever crown Duskendale lost, it can still supply the crown that replaced it with capable men.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:rosby": {
    paras: [
      "Rosby is a small, comfortable castle astride the well-traveled road between Duskendale and the capital, the kind of seat whose lords grow modestly rich simply by existing in the path of everyone else’s business without ever generating any drama of their own. In this era it offers the tale nothing more dramatic than good footing for travelers and a name on the map between two more consequential places — a minor holding content, as minor holdings on good roads usually are, to let history happen a day’s ride in either direction.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:antlers": {
    paras: [
      "The Antlers sits in the hills north of King’s Landing, seat of House Buckwell, whose rack-of-antlers sigil predates the Baratheon stag by a margin the family is known to mention more often than strictly necessary. Close enough to the capital to feel every tremor of royal politics and too modest to do much about any of them, Buckwell lords have traditionally practiced the small holding’s essential survival skill of swearing early and swearing sincerely to whichever direction the wind from King’s Landing seems to be blowing that season — a policy that has kept the Antlers standing through worse decades than this one.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Riverlands =================

  "loc:whitewalls": {
    paras: [
      "Whitewalls, better known as the Milkhouse, is what a dairy fortune looks like once it decides to become architecture: marble walls the color of cream, a sept glowing with colored glass, and a hall raised by Lord Ambrose Butterwell with more ambition than any single lord’s reputation could comfortably support. It stands for barely a generation before the tale catches up with it, and the occasion that draws every eye to the Gods Eye shore is, on its face, entirely ordinary — Lord Butterwell’s second marriage, to a young daughter of the Freys of the Crossing, celebrated with a tourney and a dragon’s egg offered up as the champion’s prize.",
      "Beneath the wedding feast, though, is the era’s last serious throw of the Blackfyre dice. The guest list at Whitewalls reads like a roster of men with unfinished business from Redgrass Field — Lord Gormon Peake chief among them — and among the hedge knights drawn by the joust and the egg is a mystery knight whose true face and true blood turn out to matter a great deal more than his blank shield suggested. Dunk and Egg arrive as guests, not conspirators, and find themselves, not for the last time, standing rather closer to a rebellion’s beating heart than either of them planned.",
      "The rebellion collapses the way Bloodraven prefers his rebellions to collapse — quietly, and before a single ordered battle can be fought. By the time the plot is exposed, the dragon’s egg has vanished, the pretender is dealt with, and Aerys I’s Hand has an armed host around Whitewalls’ walls by first light, having watched the whole gathering assemble itself for his convenience. It is intelligence work as decisive as any battle, and it costs the crown almost nothing next to what a real war for the throne would have.",
      "The crown’s sentence on the castle afterward is its own kind of message: Whitewalls is attainted and pulled down, marble wall by marble wall, until nothing remains that could ever again gather so many discontented men under one roof unnoticed. Travelers along the Gods Eye in later years know the site only as a scatter of pale, quarried stone — a dairy lord’s palace reduced, within a decade of its raising, to good building material for other men’s barns.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:harrenhal": {
    paras: [
      "Harrenhal was built to be the largest castle in Westeros and became, on the very first night it mattered, a monument to the folly of building anything too large to defend against a dragon. Harren the Black spent forty years of slave labor and a river of gold raising walls meant to outlast every enemy he could imagine; Aegon’s dragons burned him, his sons, and his garrison alive inside those walls in a single evening, and every family that has held the ruin since has come, sooner or later, to a bad end that the smallfolk are only too glad to blame on the castle itself rather than on ordinary bad luck.",
      "In Dunk and Egg’s age that grim tenancy belongs to House Lothston, and Lady Danelle — called, not affectionately, the Lady of the Black Bat — does nothing to discourage the whispers that cling to Harrenhal like damp. Whether the rumors of witchery are true matters less than the fact that they are believed without much argument; a castle with Harrenhal’s history does not get the benefit of the doubt, and neither, by association, does whoever is unlucky enough to hold it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:pennytree": {
    paras: [
      "Pennytree is barely more than a crossroads village on the Red Fork, notable chiefly for the great old oak at its center studded with pennies hammered in by generations of travelers hoping for luck on the road ahead — nobody in the village can say anymore who started the custom, only that stopping it now would feel like inviting bad fortune on purpose. It gave its name to a hedge knight of no particular birth, Ser Arlan of Pennytree, who took the village as his own the way landless knights sometimes borrow a place to stand behind instead of a father’s name.",
      "Ser Arlan died on the road toward Ashford, an old man whose knighthood his last squire could never quite verify and never once doubted, and it is that squire — a huge, gutter-born boy with a grave to dig and no other plan — who becomes Ser Duncan the Tall. Pennytree itself never appears again in the tales after lending its name to the opening pages, but the coin-studded oak is, in its way, the truest monument the story ever raises to Ser Arlan: an old, worn thing that keeps travelers a little braver than they’d otherwise be.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:riverrun": {
    paras: [
      "Riverrun sits where the Tumblestone meets the Red Fork, its walls washed by two rivers on three sides, ancestral seat of House Tully since Aegon the Conqueror rewarded their early submission with lordship over the whole Trident — an inheritance that cost the older riverlands houses, the Blackwoods and Brackens among them, a status they have never entirely forgiven the Tullys for receiving instead of them. In Dunk and Egg’s era Riverrun functions mostly as the quiet fact of overlordship behind the riverlands’ noisier local quarrels — the ultimate court of appeal that houses like Blackwood and Bracken, Butterwell and Frey, answer to without the tales ever needing to show the answering.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:raventree-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Raventree Hall belongs to House Blackwood, keepers of the old gods in a riverlands otherwise gone over to the Faith of the Seven generations ago, their godswood’s black-barked weirwoods as much a statement of stubborn identity as of religion. The house carries a closer tie to the era’s great events than its remote godswood might suggest — Brynden Rivers, Bloodraven himself, is Blackwood on his mother’s side, a bloodline the Blackwoods mention with considerably more pride now that their kinsman runs the Iron Throne’s intelligence network than they might have when he was simply one of the old king’s many bastards. Their ancient feud with the Brackens across the river burns on regardless, old gods against new grievances, largely undisturbed by whichever Targaryen currently sits the throne.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:stone-hedge": {
    paras: [
      "Stone Hedge answers Raventree Hall from the opposite bank of the Red Fork, seat of House Bracken and the Blackwoods’ hereditary enemy in a feud so old neither side entirely remembers the original grievance, only the centuries of raided cattle and burned fields piled on top of it. The Brackens claim their own kinship to the era’s great and terrible men — Aegor Rivers, called Bittersteel, the Blackfyre loyalists’ fiercest commander, is Bracken on his mother’s side, a mirror to Bloodraven’s Blackwood blood that turns the ancient riverlands rivalry into an uncomfortable echo of the larger dynastic war being fought, on and off, across the whole realm.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:stoney-sept": {
    paras: [
      "Stoney Sept is an unglamorous market town of the southern riverlands, known well enough for its bells that travelers use the sound to mark the hour without needing to ask, and known to Dunk and Egg specifically for a grimmer piece of business witnessed on their road north to Whitewalls — the hanging of Wyl brigands, marcher outlaws whose family seat lies far south in the Dornish borderlands, executed here for crimes committed well outside their own country. It is the kind of ordinary roadside justice that barely rates a paragraph in most tellings, and its purpose in the Mystery Knight is exactly that ordinariness: a plain reminder, before the story turns toward a conspiracy of lords and princes, of the coarser justice meted out to men without titles to protect them.",
      "The town itself has no larger role to play in this era — its later fame in the wars still generations off, unknown and unknowable to anyone drinking in its inns in 212 AC — and that is rather the point of including it at all. Stoney Sept is where the realm’s low business gets conducted while its high business gathers itself at Whitewalls a few days’ ride further on, and Dunk and Egg pass through it as travelers rather than participants, watching the rope do its work before moving on toward the rope that very nearly waits for them too.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:acorn-hall": {
    paras: [
      "Acorn Hall is a snug forest keep in the riverlands’ southern foothills, seat of House Smallwood, a family whose chief historical distinction is a comfortable absence of historical distinction — no great feuds, no rebellions backed or betrayed, simply generations of minor lordship managed competently enough that the wider realm has never had cause to learn their name. In an age defined by the loud failures of prouder houses, Acorn Hall’s quiet, uneventful loyalty reads almost as its own kind of achievement.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:maidenpool": {
    paras: [
      "Maidenpool takes its name and its whole romantic reputation from a single scene out of the oldest songs: Florian the Fool first glimpsing Jonquil bathing with her sisters in the pool that still draws sentimental travelers to the town’s edge, whatever the truth of a jester who supposedly loved a highborn lady enough to become a knight for her. House Mooton holds the harbor above with the practiced ease of river lords who have learned, across centuries of passing armies and passing fashions, that surviving is a skill in its own right. In Dunk and Egg’s era the town is simply a working port on the Bay of Crabs — the songs older and gentler than anything the realm’s current politics have on offer.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:the-twins": {
    paras: [
      "The Twins are two matched castles joined by a single bridge across the Green Fork, and House Frey has made an entire family fortune out of controlling the crossing — every merchant, army, and wedding party that wants to move quickly between the riverlands’ north and south pays the toll one way or another, in coin if not always in courtesy. It is to Whitewalls that the current Lord Frey travels in the Mystery Knight, delivering a daughter to wed Lord Butterwell and, tagging along for the occasion, a small and startlingly sharp-tongued heir who spends the wedding making a thorough nuisance of himself among guests considerably more important than a four-year-old has any right to irritate.",
      "That child is worth a second look, because the tales are quietly, deliberately introducing him: the same boy who mocks his betters at Whitewalls without a flicker of shame will grow, across the coming decades, into the Lord Frey the realm eventually learns to fear rather than laugh at — a man whose sense of a slight, once earned, is never, ever allowed to lapse. The Twins in this era are simply a well-tolled bridge; what the family becomes, the wedding at Whitewalls has already shown a glimpse of, to anyone paying attention to the noisy little boy underfoot.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:seagard": {
    paras: [
      "Seagard is a fortified port on the sunset coast, raised generations ago by House Mallister expressly to answer the ironborn’s habit of treating the riverlands’ western shore as an open larder, and the castle has rarely had less use for its walls than it would like in Dunk and Egg’s day. Dagon Greyjoy’s longships are reaving the western seas with a boldness the crown has not yet managed to answer decisively, and Seagard’s watch — old duty, freshly urgent again — has gone from ceremonial to constant, its garrison busier this decade than in the several quiet ones before it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:high-heart": {
    paras: [
      "High Heart is a bald, wind-scoured hill in the heart of the riverlands, ringed by the stumps of weirwoods the Andals cut down in an older, uglier age of conquest — a wound in the landscape that has never quite healed over, and a place the smallfolk still route their wagons well around after dark. It was sacred to the children of the forest and to the First Men who came after them, and whatever exactly haunts it now, the local conviction that something does has outlasted every King's Landing century since the trees themselves were felled. In Dunk and Egg’s era it remains simply what it has always been to travelers on the roads nearby: a landmark best admired from a comfortable distance, and crossed, if at all, well before sundown.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Vale =================

  "loc:the-eyrie": {
    paras: [
      "The Eyrie sits atop the Giant’s Lance on a perch so sheer that Aegon’s dragons never had to burn it — the Arryns who ruled the Vale as Kings of Mountain and Vale for thousands of years surrendered peacefully once Visenya Targaryen simply flew up to the castle gate on dragonback and asked them to, sparing everyone the pointless exercise of testing whether the mountain could actually be taken by force. That bloodless conquest set the tone for how the Vale has always understood its own defenses: not walls, particularly, but altitude, and a goat path below narrow enough that an army trying to climb it arrives, if it arrives at all, exhausted and single-file.",
      "In Dunk and Egg’s age the castle remains what it was under Visenya — a seat that answers to the crown in name and to its own geography in every practical sense, ruled by House Arryn from white stone halls the sky itself seems to guard better than any garrison could.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:gulltown": {
    paras: [
      "Gulltown is the Vale’s working harbor, a trading city held for the Arryns by House Grafton, whose fortunes rise and fall with shipping tolls and salt-fish prices rather than with the mountain politics that occupy the Eyrie above. It is, in effect, the Vale’s door to the wider realm — the Eyrie handles the Vale’s pride, Gulltown handles its commerce — and in an era when the crown’s peace has made travel and trade unusually safe, that door does brisker business than it has in a generation.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:runestone": {
    paras: [
      "Runestone is the ancestral seat of House Royce, whose armor — bronze, graven with runes older than the Andal invasion — is the family’s proudest boast and, not incidentally, physical proof that they were lords in the Vale before the Andals arrived to reshuffle everyone else’s claims. Where most of the Vale’s great houses trace themselves to Andal conquerors, the Royces trade on being First Men who simply never lost, and in Dunk and Egg’s era that ancient pedigree still buys them a respect at court that younger, richer houses can only envy.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Westerlands =================

  "loc:casterly-rock": {
    paras: [
      "Casterly Rock is a fortress carved directly into a golden headland above the Sunset Sea, seat of the wealthiest house in the Seven Kingdoms and, by the smallfolk’s enduring account, once home to a family of Casterlys the Lannisters supposedly tricked or outlasted out of the mountain generations before any record can confirm it. The gold that funds the family’s legend still comes out of the Rock’s own mines in Dunk and Egg’s era, and House Lannister’s wealth buys them a presence at every great occasion the realm holds — Ser Tybolt Lannister rides among the champions at Ashford Meadow, one gilded name among many drawn to a tourney that briefly outshone even the West’s reputation for splendor.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:lannisport": {
    paras: [
      "Lannisport is the walled port city sheltering in Casterly Rock’s shadow, wealthy enough in the ordinary run of things to be the pride of the western coast — which makes its sack within living memory, at the hands of Dagon Greyjoy’s longships, a humiliation the West has not finished swallowing. That the richest house in Westeros could not keep raiders off its own harbor is the sort of insult money cannot quite paper over, and Lannisport’s current garrisons, considerably more alert than they were before the raid, are the visible scar of a lesson the Lannisters intend never to need learning twice.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:golden-tooth": {
    paras: [
      "Golden Tooth is a small, strongly built castle commanding the hill road between the riverlands and the westerlands, held by House Lefford in a position that matters far more than the castle’s modest size would suggest — every army, merchant train, or wedding party moving between the two regions by land passes beneath its walls, and the Leffords have made a comfortable living out of being the gate nobody can simply walk around.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:crakehall": {
    paras: [
      "Crakehall takes its boar for a sigil seriously enough that the house is known, even at some distance, for a hunting culture as fierce as its heraldry — an heir raised here is expected to prove his nerve against tusks and not merely against a tourney lance. That martial bent serves the family well at court: Ser Roland Crakehall wears the white cloak of the Kingsguard at Ashford Meadow, one more instance of a westerlands house turning its rough country pastimes into royal service.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The North =================

  "loc:winterfell": {
    paras: [
      "Winterfell in this era belongs to Lord Beron Stark, and the ancient stronghold of the Kings of Winter sits, as it usually does, at a considerable remove from whatever tourney or wedding is currently absorbing the south’s attention — the North’s politics run on a different clock, measured in seasons and reavers rather than in successions and trials of seven. Lord Beron’s more pressing business in these years is driving Dagon Greyjoy’s longships off his own shores, a duty considerably less romantic than anything happening at Ashford Meadow but, to the fisherfolk actually being raided, a great deal more urgent.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:white-harbor": {
    paras: [
      "White Harbor is the North’s only true city, a clean port of pale stone at the mouth of the White Knife, held by House Manderly — a family whose ancestors migrated north from the Reach so many centuries ago, fleeing troubles long since forgotten by everyone but their own family history, that no Northman living remembers them as anything but thoroughly, comfortably native. In Dunk and Egg’s era the city carries on its usual business of trading southern goods for northern timber and furs, a warmer, busier counterpoint to Winterfell’s colder formality a few days’ ride inland.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:moat-cailin": {
    paras: [
      "Moat Cailin’s three crumbling towers command the narrow causeway through the Neck’s swamps, and though centuries of decay have reduced what was once a formidable First Men fortress to a broken shell, the ruin still does the one job that has always mattered most: it makes the North extraordinarily expensive to invade by land. The Andals never conquered the North in the first place, and historians have generally credited Moat Cailin and the crannogmen’s swamps around it at least as much as Northern steel for that unbroken record — an old lock, badly rusted, that nobody has yet needed to test seriously.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:castle-black": {
    paras: [
      "Castle Black anchors the Wall’s middle stretch, chief fortress of a Night’s Watch that has spent the long, mostly peaceful decades of Daeron II’s and Aerys I’s reigns quietly shrinking — fewer criminals sentenced to the black, fewer younger sons with nothing better to do, and a border duty that feels, to a realm at peace with itself, more like ceremony than necessity. That complacency is precisely what makes the Watch’s decline dangerous rather than merely sad: an institution runs thinnest exactly when the era around it has stopped bothering to ask what it's still guarding against, and Castle Black’s garrison in this age is smaller, by any measure, than the job description on its gate.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Iron Islands =================

  "loc:pyke": {
    paras: [
      "Pyke is a castle of black stone strung across storm-battered sea-stacks by rope bridges that would unsettle a mainland lord on his best day, seat of House Greyjoy and their old creed that a ship earns more honor than a plow. Dagon Greyjoy currently tests exactly how far that creed can be indulged under a crown determined to keep its peace — his longships reave the sunset coasts from Lannisport to Seagard while the rest of the Seven Kingdoms enjoys the long, comfortable calm Daeron II’s reign is remembered for, a discrepancy the Iron Throne has noticed and not yet found convenient to correct.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= Dorne =================

  "loc:sunspear": {
    paras: [
      "Sunspear’s winding, sand-colored walls house the one triumph of Daeron II’s reign large enough to earn him the epithet the Good outright: a Dornish peace bought not with dragons or armies, both of which had failed his predecessors repeatedly across a century of war, but with two marriages — his own sister wed to the Prince of Dorne, and Daeron himself taking a Dornish princess to wife. House Martell’s seat, joined to the Seven Kingdoms at last by contract rather than conquest, stands in Dunk and Egg’s era as the living proof that Daeron’s patient diplomacy accomplished what generations of his ancestors’ war elephants — sorry, dragons — never could.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:yronwood": {
    paras: [
      "Yronwood styles itself the Bloodroyal and holds, by most reckonings, the proudest seat in Dorne after Sunspear itself — wardens of the Boneway, and rivals to House Martell for the crown of Dorne back in the centuries before the Martells finally secured it for good. That old rivalry has cooled into mere prickliness by Daeron II’s reign, but Yronwood lords still take visible pleasure in reminding visitors that Martell primacy in Dorne was a historical outcome, not a natural law.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:starfall": {
    paras: [
      "Starfall stands on the Torentine, ancestral seat of House Dayne and keepers of Dawn, a pale sword said to be forged from the heart of a fallen star and carried only by whichever Dayne proves worthy of the title Sword of the Morning — a hereditary honor bestowed, not automatically inherited, which keeps the house's greatest legend permanently, usefully unresolved. In an age when most great houses measure their worth in acres and armies, Starfall's whole prestige rests instead on a sword and the rare men judged fit to wield it.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:vaith": {
    paras: [
      "Vaith is a river seat amid Dorne’s red sands, a minor holding of House Vaith notable in these tales mainly as a destination rather than a setting — the place Dunk and Egg mean to travel toward when the road their creator actually finished writing runs out. It stands, appropriately for a place the story never quite reaches, as a small monument to the tales GRRM left unwritten: real on the map, waiting on the page.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:wyl": {
    paras: [
      "Wyl is a marcher seat deep in the Boneway, old in border blood and home country to the very brigands hanged at Stoney Sept on Dunk and Egg's road to Whitewalls — a house whose relationship with the crown's law has, across generations of raiding the stormlands passes, run rather warmer toward its own outlaws than toward the crown's justice. Wyl produces knights and lords like any other house; it has simply never been especially embarrassed about the family's other trade as well.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= Across the narrow sea =================

  "loc:tyrosh": {
    paras: [
      "Tyrosh is an island Free City famous for the outlandishly dyed beards of its wealthier citizens and, less decoratively, for the hired swords the city has always been willing to sell to whoever's coin is heaviest. It is also, in Dunk and Egg's era, the natural home for the losing side of a civil war: Aegor Rivers, called Bittersteel, gathered the Blackfyre loyalists who survived Redgrass Field and built them into the Golden Company, a mercenary force with no country to serve but plenty of old grievance to spend on somebody else's throne.",
      "From Tyrosh and cities like it, the black dragon's cause never fully dies even a generation after Daemon Blackfyre himself fell at Redgrass Field — his surviving sons and their partisans plot, recruit, and wait across the water for an opening, and it is exactly this patient exile network that eventually produces the Whitewalls conspiracy. Tyrosh's real export, in this telling, is not dye or swords-for-hire but a rebellion kept perpetually, expensively alive by men with nowhere else to go home to.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:lys": {
    paras: [
      "Lys is an island Free City built almost entirely around pleasure — perfumed gardens, famous courtesans, a reputation for indulgence that draws exiles and wastrels from every kingdom that has ever needed somewhere discreet to send an embarrassment. Prince Aerion Targaryen, called Brightflame by nobody but himself, is dispatched here in gilded disgrace after the incident at Ashford Meadow, his father Maekar apparently reasoning that a cruel, vain prince causes less damage spending his family's money on Lys's pleasures than he would loitering closer to the succession.",
      "Lys gets rather the worse end of that bargain than the Targaryens do. Aerion's stay abroad does nothing to cure whatever runs wrong in him — if anything the years away seem to deepen his conviction, half poetry and half madness, that he is meant for something more than mortal — and when he eventually returns to Westeros he acts on a delusion that a cup of wildfire might transform a man into a dragon outright. It does not, of course; it only kills him, horribly and rather publicly, and clears a space in the succession that nobody engineering the Whitewalls plot anticipated needing to plan around. Lys, for its part, is left to explain to visiting Westerosi lords for years afterward that no, it did not put the idea in his head — he arrived that way.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:pentos": {
    paras: [
      "Pentos is a wealthy Free City of canals and colored domes, governed by a council of magisters whose fortunes run on trade with Westeros rather than on the narrow sea's older, murkier business of exiles and mercenary companies. Unlike Tyrosh or Lys, Pentos keeps itself carefully uninvolved in the Blackfyre exiles' scheming in Dunk and Egg's era — a magister's wealth depends on being trusted by whoever eventually wins any given quarrel, and Pentosi merchants have found it considerably more profitable to sell wine and cloth to both sides of a Westerosi dispute than to bet on either one.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:braavos": {
    paras: [
      "Braavos is the Free Cities' best-kept secret even by its own account — a fog-shrouded lagoon city founded generations ago by slaves who fled Valyria's dragonlords and their fire, hidden behind the colossal bronze Titan that guards its harbor mouth from anyone who hasn't earned the welcome. Its most consequential export to Westeros in this era is not silk or steel but credit: the Iron Bank of Braavos has already built the reputation it will spend the next century trading on, lending to kings who need money and collecting, eventually, from kings who would very much rather it didn't.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Reach: the rest =================

  "loc:goldengrove": {
    paras: [
      "Goldengrove sits among golden-leaved trees in the northern Reach, seat of House Rowan, a family old enough to claim lordship in the region predating even the Gardener kings the Tyrells eventually succeeded — a boast the Rowans have had many centuries to refine and no particular reason to stop making. As liege lords over both Standfast and Coldmoat, Goldengrove is technically the court of last resort for the water dispute that nearly burns Wat's Wood to the ground in the Sworn Sword, though Lady Rohanne and Ser Eustace settle their own account before it ever needs escalating that far — leaving Goldengrove's authority, in this instance, entirely theoretical and entirely sufficient anyway.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:old-oak": {
    paras: [
      "Old Oak is an ancient seat on the sea road north of Highgarden, home to House Oakheart, a family whose sons have traditionally gravitated toward the Kingsguard's white cloak more often than most — a tradition already well established by Dunk and Egg's era, and one the house wears as a point of quiet pride rather than mere coincidence. The castle itself, old even by Reach standards, does its work mostly unremarked: guarding a stretch of coast that has generally had the good sense not to need much guarding.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:bitterbridge": {
    paras: [
      "Bitterbridge takes its name from the ancient stone span carrying the roseroad across the Mander, seat of the minor House Caswell, whose chief historical function has always been keeping that bridge in good repair for grander lords passing through on business more important than anything happening in Bitterbridge itself. In Dunk and Egg's era the bridge sees the ordinary traffic of a kingdom at peace — merchants, pilgrims, the occasional lord's retinue — rather than the armies and camp-followers it will host in ages the tales don't reach.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:tumbleton": {
    paras: [
      "Tumbleton is a market town on the upper Mander whose fields, two generations before Dunk and Egg ever walk them, hosted one of the ugliest reckonings of the Dance of the Dragons — a royalist army burned where it camped, and the survivors finished off by a plague so thorough the soldiers called it the Winter Fever rather than admit dragonfire alone had done the job. By the time the Reach's peacetime commerce reclaims the fields, Tumbleton has settled back into being what it was before that terrible season: an unremarkable stop on the Mander's trade routes, its worst history buried under decades of ordinary market days.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:honeyholt": {
    paras: [
      "Honeyholt sits amid orchards and hives on the Honeywine, seat of House Beesbury, a family whose entire heraldic identity — and, by most accounts, a fair portion of their actual income — runs on honey rather than on anything more martial. Young Ser Humfrey of Honeyholt stands among the seven who fight and bleed for Dunk's cause at Ashford Meadow, a small act of decency from a minor house that costs the Beesburys rather more than a jar of their famous honey ever could.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:horn-hill": {
    paras: [
      "Horn Hill lies beneath the foothills of the Red Mountains, seat of House Tarly, a family already known in this era for producing capable, hard-nosed commanders rather than merely decorative lords — a reputation the house has been quietly building for generations before it becomes the stuff of wider legend. In Dunk and Egg's age Horn Hill is simply one more well-run Reach holding, its martial seriousness a matter of local reputation rather than realm-wide fame just yet.",
    ],
  },

  "loc:brightwater-keep": {
    paras: [
      "Brightwater Keep is the Florents' seat, and the fox-eared house has never entirely made peace with the settlement that gave Highgarden to the Tyrells rather than to them — the Florents can trace their own descent from the Gardener kings through the female line, a claim of blood the Tyrells simply cannot match no matter how many roses they grow. That grievance has cooled into a running joke rather than an active feud by Dunk and Egg's era, but it is the sort of joke Brightwater Keep's lords tell often enough that nobody in the Reach has forgotten the punchline is, underneath, entirely sincere.",
    ],
  },

  // ================= The Stormlands: append =================
});
